UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Antony Santos, born Domingo Antonio Santos Muñoz on May 5, 1967, is a Dominican singer, guitarist, songwriter and producer, and the single most consequential figure in the transformation of bachata from a stigmatized rural music into the country’s dominant popular genre. Known as \"El Mayimbe,\" he came out of the northwestern border country playing güira behind someone else, and within three years of going solo had rewritten what bachata was allowed to sound like.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Early years and the güira","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Santos was born in Clavellinas, a rural settlement in Las Matas de Santa Cruz, Monte Cristi province, into severe poverty — his father’s work was unsteady and food was not reliable. He made his first instruments out of broken bottles before he ever had a guitar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He entered the music business in the late 1980s not as a singer but as a güirero, playing percussion behind the bachatero ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"0760875d-6b6f-4a48-8aed-6e57934d1baa","displayText":"Luis Vargas","occurrenceId":"b81e25a9-bbd2-43c5-8720-dc4831854481"}},{"text":". It was the right apprenticeship: bachata is a rhythm musician’s genre before it is a singer’s, and the years spent inside its percussion gave him an instinct for it that shows in everything he later produced himself.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La Chupadera and \"Voy pa’llá\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He went out on his own in 1991 with the album La Chupadera, and its single «Voy pa’llá» did something no bachata record had managed before: it made a bachatero of rural origin a national star, and carried into Dominican communities across the United States. The albums came fast afterward — La batalla in 1992, Corazón bonito in 1993, Corazón culpable in 1994 — and by the middle of the decade the argument about who led the genre was effectively over.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"What he changed about bachata","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The bachata Santos inherited was music of the barroom and the brothel, sung in a rough register about drinking and betrayal, and looked down on accordingly. What he brought to it was romantic vulnerability — a man singing plainly about hurt rather than performing toughness about it — and that shift did more to make the genre respectable than any amount of advocacy could have.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He changed how it sounded, too. He brought in saxophone, keyboards and strings, instruments the style had never used; he was particular about guitars and effects pedals in a way bachateros had not been; and he raised the standard of production across the industry simply by clearing a higher bar himself. He also leaned hard on merengue in his records, which mattered strategically: Dominicans who still considered bachata beneath them were already comfortable dancing merengue, and he used that comfort as a door.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The trinity","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Santos, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"0760875d-6b6f-4a48-8aed-6e57934d1baa","displayText":"Luis Vargas","occurrenceId":"d3488af9-7f08-4402-8584-33b93342d7fc"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"96e69c00-dbb0-4cb4-ab48-ea46be9c4591","displayText":"Raulín Rodríguez","occurrenceId":"e924cfb7-f954-4e12-bc25-2fdc111cf328"}},{"text":" all came from the same stretch of the northwestern border and are spoken of together as the trinity of Dominican bachata. His relationship with Vargas in particular ran as a long public rivalry, which sold records for both of them for years. In 2011 the three appeared together on «Debate de 4», a ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"8f1d2a44-3c6e-4b17-9a58-7d0e5c9b21f3","displayText":"Romeo Santos","occurrenceId":"03d0f7f8-2aa4-494e-8e90-cfc344d30ac7"}},{"text":" recording that put the generation that built modern bachata on one track — a summit the genre had never staged before.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Recognition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The honors accumulated across three decades:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"five Casandra/Soberano awards as Bachatero del Año, in 1996, 2007, 2009, 2013 and 2019,","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"the Gran Soberano in 2019, the top lifetime honor in Dominican arts,","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"the first solo Madison Square Garden concert by a traditional bachatero, in 2014,","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"number one on Billboard’s Tropical Airplay chart with «Solo te amo» in 2014,","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"and August 8 proclaimed \"Anthony Santos Day\" in New York City in 2019.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He is the second Dominican musician to carry the title \"El Mayimbe\" — a Taíno word for a village chief — after the merengue singer ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"b5e93116-687a-4572-bbc9-a65c7be126cd"}},{"text":"; he